4:
\textbf{(a)}
\[\lim_{x\to0^-}f=2(0)+3=3,\quad \lim_{x\to0^+}f=\frac{4}{0+1}=4,\quad \lim_{x\to1^-}f=\frac42=2,\quad \lim_{x\to1^+}f=2\sqrt1=2.\]
\textbf{(b)} At $x=0$ the one-sided limits differ (3 vs 4): a \textbf{jump} discontinuity. At $x=1$ both limits equal $f(1)=\frac42=2$, so $f$ is continuous there. Each piece is continuous on its own interval. Only discontinuity: \boxed{x=0\ \text{(jump)}}.

\textbf{(c)}
\[f'(x)=\begin{cases}2, & x<0\\[2pt] -\dfrac{4}{(x+1)^2}, & 0<x<1\\[6pt] \dfrac{1}{\sqrt x}, & x>1\end{cases}\]
\textbf{(d)} $x=0$: not differentiable because $f$ is discontinuous there. $x=1$: $f$ is continuous, but the left derivative is $-\frac{4}{4}=-1$ and the right derivative is $\frac{1}{1}=1$, so there is a \textbf{corner}. Not differentiable at \boxed{x=0\text{ and }x=1}.

